EXPERIMENT 8: DEPLOY A STATIC WEBSITE USING GITHUB ACTIONS AND GITHUB PAGES

AIM:
To create a static website and deploy it automatically using GitHub Actions and GitHub Pages.

REPOSITORY:
https://github.com/sivapriyakumar246-cyber/GitExperiment1

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 1: OPEN THE REPOSITORY IN CMD
--------------------------------------------------

If the repository is already cloned on the lab computer, run:

cd "%USERPROFILE%\GitExperiment1"

Explanation:
This command moves CMD into the GitExperiment1 project folder.

Get the latest repository files:

git pull --no-rebase origin main

Explanation:
This downloads and merges the latest changes from GitHub.

If the repository is NOT cloned on the lab computer, run these instead:

git clone https://github.com/sivapriyakumar246-cyber/GitExperiment1.git
cd GitExperiment1

Explanation:
git clone downloads the repository from GitHub.
cd enters the downloaded project folder.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 2: CREATE THE WEBSITE FILE
--------------------------------------------------

Run this command in CMD:

notepad index.html

Explanation:
This opens Notepad to create or edit the website's HTML file.

PASTE THE FOLLOWING HTML INTO NOTEPAD:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Static Website</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to My Website!</h1>
    <p>This website is deployed automatically using GitHub Actions.</p>
</body>
</html>

Save the file using Ctrl+S and close Notepad.

Explanation:
index.html contains the webpage content that visitors will see.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 3: CREATE THE GITHUB ACTIONS WORKFLOW
--------------------------------------------------

Run these commands in CMD:

mkdir .github\workflows
notepad .github\workflows\deploy.yml

Explanation:
mkdir creates the workflow folder if it does not already exist.
If CMD says the folder already exists, continue.
notepad opens the YAML workflow file.

PASTE THE FOLLOWING YAML INTO NOTEPAD:

name: Deploy Static Site

on:
  push:
    branches: ["main"]

permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write

jobs:
  build-and-deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    environment:
      name: github-pages
      url: ${{ steps.deployment.outputs.page_url }}

    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Configure GitHub Pages
        uses: actions/configure-pages@v5
        with:
          enablement: true

      - name: Upload website files
        uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v4
        with:
          path: .

      - name: Deploy website
        id: deployment
        uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4

Save using Ctrl+S and close Notepad.

Explanation:
This workflow runs whenever changes are pushed to the main branch.
It checks out the repository, prepares GitHub Pages, uploads the website files,
and deploys the website.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 4: ENABLE GITHUB PAGES
--------------------------------------------------

Run this command in CMD to open the Pages settings:

start https://github.com/sivapriyakumar246-cyber/GitExperiment1/settings/pages

Explanation:
This opens the repository's GitHub Pages settings.

On the GitHub Pages page:
1. Find the Source dropdown under Build and deployment.
2. Select GitHub Actions.
3. If GitHub Actions is already selected, leave it selected.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 5: ADD, COMMIT, AND PUSH THE FILES
--------------------------------------------------

Run these commands in CMD:

git add index.html .github\workflows\deploy.yml

Explanation:
This stages the website file and the deployment workflow.

Check the staged changes:

git status

Explanation:
This displays the files that are ready to be committed.

Commit the changes:

git commit -m "Deploy static website using GitHub Actions"

Explanation:
This saves the staged changes as a Git commit.

Push the commit to GitHub:

git push origin main

Explanation:
This uploads the commit to the main branch on GitHub.
The push triggers the deployment workflow.

If Git says "nothing to commit", check git status.
If the push is rejected because GitHub has newer commits, run:

git pull --no-rebase origin main
git push origin main

If a merge conflict appears, stop and resolve it before pushing.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 6: CHECK THE GITHUB ACTIONS WORKFLOW
--------------------------------------------------

Run this command in CMD:

start https://github.com/sivapriyakumar246-cyber/GitExperiment1/actions

Explanation:
This opens the GitHub Actions page.

1. Find the Deploy Static Site workflow.
2. Open its latest run.
3. Wait for it to finish.
4. A green check mark means the deployment succeeded.
5. If it fails, open the failed job and read the error.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 7: OPEN THE DEPLOYED WEBSITE
--------------------------------------------------

Run this command in CMD:

start https://sivapriyakumar246-cyber.github.io/GitExperiment1/

Explanation:
This opens the published website in the browser.

EXPECTED OUTPUT:
The webpage displays:

Welcome to My Website!
This website is deployed automatically using GitHub Actions.

--------------------------------------------------
RESULT:
--------------------------------------------------

The static website was successfully deployed using GitHub Actions and GitHub Pages.
The deployment workflow completed successfully, and the published website displayed
"Welcome to My Website!".
